<%# The full text of an entry: the intro, the body, and the affiliate-link disclosure. The article
    template and the page template share it. `pagefind`, which is an optional local, adds the
    data-pagefind-body attribute, which lets the site search index the div. `track_body_links`,
    which is an optional local, adds the Article Click analytics to each link to an article.
    ⚠️ A local must not have the name of a helper. Without the local, defined? finds the helper and
    the code calls it. %>
<% link_section = (MarkupHelpers::ARTICLE_BODY_SECTION if defined?(track_body_links) && track_body_links) %>
<div class="entry__body"<%= ' data-pagefind-body'.html_safe if defined?(pagefind) && pagefind %>>
  <%# first_image marks the FIRST image of the two calls only. Refer to claim_lcp_image.
      ⚠️ :priority, and NOT :eager. A measurement gave the LCP element of an article as text: the
      first body image of a race report is at 2531px, and the fold is at 664px. Thus the image
      stays lazy, and the mark only raises the priority of the fetch that the browser starts. %>
  <%= render_body(article.intro, image_variant: :full, first_image: :priority, link_section: link_section) %>
  <%= render_body(article.body, image_variant: :full, first_image: :priority, link_section: link_section) %>
  <%# The contact form is a partial, and it is not raw HTML in the Contentful body. SmartyPants, in
      render_body, makes the quotation marks in a raw HTML attribute curly, and that breaks the
      form. Refer to partials/_contact_form. %>
  <%= partial 'partials/contact_form' if article.slug == 'contact' %>
  <%= partial 'partials/disclosure', locals: { entry: article } %>
</div>
